2026-08-16T11:00:46Z    [HD:Net sales] GATE identity: comparable sales -25.0875% is implausible for a mature retailer absent an exceptional event
2026-08-16T11:00:46Z    [HD:Net sales] GATE critic: The growth input “FY2023->FY2024 +24.1%” is wrong for Home Depot’s net sales; the company did not post anything close to +24% annual sales growth in that period (reported results were roughly flat to down low single digits). Including this erroneous +24.1% inflates your median YoY growth to 1.71%; dropping it brings the median to about 0.6%, implying FY2026Q2 ≈ 45,548 USDm rather than 46,052.5 USDm. The forecast is therefore biased upward by a mis-stated prior-year growth figure.
2026-08-16T11:01:36Z    [HD:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE identity: comparable sales -25.0875% is implausible for a mature retailer absent an exceptional event
2026-08-16T11:01:36Z    [HD:Adjusted diluted EPS] GATE critic: Wrong basis: you start from an adjusted diluted EPS figure for FY2025Q2 ($4.68) but one of the two YoY growth inputs you apply (FY2025Q1→FY2026Q1: −3.7%) is based on reported diluted EPS (GAAP), not adjusted. Applying a GAAP growth rate to an adjusted EPS base yields a mismatched-basis estimate for FY2026Q2. This can be checked by comparing HD’s FY2026 Q1 release (YoY change is for GAAP diluted EPS) against the FY2025 Q2 figure you cite as “adjusted.”
2026-08-16T11:02:04Z    [HD:Comparable sales, total company] GATE identity: comparable sales -25.0875% is implausible for a mature retailer absent an exceptional event
2026-08-16T11:02:04Z    [HD:Comparable sales, total company] GATE critic: You treated the FY2025Q2 input as −40% comparable sales based on the line “foreign exchange rates negatively impacted total company comparable sales by approximately 40 basis points.” That figure is 40 bps (≈−0.40%), not −40%, and it refers to the FX impact component, not the total comparable sales. Using −40 instead of −0.40 drives your average to −25.09% by a two-orders-of-magnitude unit error on a non-comparable measure.
